# Who reviews what.
#
# This repository moves at two speeds, and the split is deliberate.
#
# The framework at the root is a published specification under CC BY 4.0. People
# cite it, adapt it, and build against it, so its rules change rarely and never
# by accident. Every framework path here requires the maintainer's review.
#
# The platform is software. It should move quickly, and a second pair of hands
# on it is the point. It carries no owner line below, so the repository's normal
# review rules apply.
#
# GitHub applies the LAST matching pattern, so the specific rules sit at the
# bottom.

# Default: the maintainer sees everything until the review habit settles.
*                               @gfitzp79

# -- the framework: stable, cited, changed on purpose ----------------------
/specification/                 @gfitzp79
/architecture/                  @gfitzp79
/methodology/                   @gfitzp79
/appendix.md                    @gfitzp79
/disclaimer.md                  @gfitzp79
/LICENSE                        @gfitzp79
/platform/LICENSE               @gfitzp79
/platform/NOTICE                @gfitzp79

# Licensing, security policy and the contribution rules themselves.
/SECURITY.md                    @gfitzp79
/CONTRIBUTING.md                @gfitzp79
/.github/                       @gfitzp79

# The checks that keep the specification and the code honest. Weakening one of
# these is a governance change wearing an infrastructure hat.
/tools/                         @gfitzp79

# The operating model shipped as a worked example, and the framework catalogues
# whose licensing AINV-6 enforces.
/platform/config/               @gfitzp79

# -- the platform: the part a contributor should be able to move -----------
#
# Left unowned on purpose. Everything under platform/ other than the paths
# above follows the repository's normal review settings.
